{"name":"Toile Blue","hex":"#94bee1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"toile","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plain or simple twilled fabric","a draft garment made of inexpensive cloth for the purposes of fitting and design evaluation"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/toile"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Toile","blocks":[{"text":"Toile is a textile fabric comparable to fine batiste with a cloth weave. Natural silk or chemical fiber filaments are usually used as materials. The word toile can refer to the fabric itself or to a test garment sewn from calico. The French term toile entered the English language around the 12th century, was used in the Middle Ages and has since disappeared."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Handkerchief.jpg/330px-Handkerchief.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toile","wikidata":"Q1422692"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Printeddress.jpg/500px-Printeddress.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Printeddress.jpg/500px-Printeddress.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Dress from Toile de Jouy","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APrinteddress.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Le_studio_d%27Yves_Saint_Laurent_%28Mus%C3%A9e_Yves_Saint_Laurent%2C_Paris%29_-_31940295547.jpg/500px-Le_studio_d%27Yves_Saint_Laurent_%28Mus%C3%A9e_Yves_Saint_Laurent%2C_Paris%29_-_31940295547.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Le_studio_d%27Yves_Saint_Laurent_%28Mus%C3%A9e_Yves_Saint_Laurent%2C_Paris%29_-_31940295547.jpg/1280px-Le_studio_d%27Yves_Saint_Laurent_%28Mus%C3%A9e_Yves_Saint_Laurent%2C_Paris%29_-_31940295547.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Le_studio_d%27Yves_Saint_Laurent_%28Mus%C3%A9e_Yves_Saint_Laurent%2C_Paris%29_-_31940295547.jpg/500px-Le_studio_d%27Yves_Saint_Laurent_%28Mus%C3%A9e_Yves_Saint_Laurent%2C_Paris%29_-_31940295547.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Yves Saint Laurent's studio, showing a toile for a safari jacket","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALe_studio_d'Yves_Saint_Laurent_(Mus%C3%A9e_Yves_Saint_Lau